A||||14||bcwawright||Electromotive TEC||||5||
Z||000000||bcwawright||05-14-2001||07:22 PM||||I have a 88GT with an IRM turbo installed and was wondering if anyone has used Electromotive's TEC system. If not I guess I'm on my own.<BR>Thanks for any help.<P>88GT Turbo F355 Replica||||reg||5
Z||000001||terryk||05-14-2001||07:57 PM||||Why would you use it?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Slammed Fiero||05-14-2001||10:06 PM||||Electromotive TEC 2 systems (I assume your talking tech2) are great for tuning a car. We had one on a Fuel injected Corvair , what do you need to know?? Basically what we did was spent a lot of time at the dyno , But found about 22hp @ the wheels with the Tech unit , something that is quite amazing considering it couldn't have been done with the regular 280ZX Millen ECm we were using (corvair 2.9ltrs , 2.8ltr Fulie Datsun)<P><BR>what do you need to know?<P><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1607505&a=12259132&p=48230089&Sequence=0&res=high">     <BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals..Autocrossed , Tracked , abused.||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||05-14-2001||10:48 PM||||Tech 2 scanner? Made by HP. I thought you meant the aftermarket ECM unless it's a HUD for the ECM side connector.||||reg||1
Z||000004||bcwawright||05-15-2001||01:43 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B>Electromotive TEC 2 systems (I assume your talking tech2) are great for tuning a car. We had one on a Fuel injected Corvair , what do you need to know?? Basically what we did was spent a lot of time at the dyno , But found about 22hp @ the wheels with the Tech unit , something that is quite amazing considering it couldn't have been done with the regular 280ZX Millen ECm we were using (corvair 2.9ltrs , 2.8ltr Fulie Datsun)<P><BR>what do you need to know?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Thanks for the offer.<BR>I heard that I could get a significant improvement in driveability and HP. I know that the Total Engine Control unit by Electromotive does away with the stock ignition including the distributor and that a crank trigger has to be mounted....How hard is it and what is involved in mounting the trigger on a 2.8 (88GT). Is the HP gain and improved driveability worth the high price?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||bcwawright||05-15-2001||01:47 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by terryk:<BR><B>Tech 2 scanner? Made by HP. I thought you meant the aftermarket ECM unless it's a HUD for the ECM side connector.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Terryk....The Electromotive unit I'm talking about fires the plugs and the injectors....it monitors 2 or 3 sources and figures out based on these monitors what the best timing and fuel curve to produce optimum HP.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||pherder||05-15-2001||10:29 AM||||I hate having to spam all these threads (and I will stop when I either sell the unit or ?) but there is another ECM that can be used (which is what I am trying to sell!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> )<P>The Haltech E6GM computer will use the <B>STOCK</B> Fiero's V6 wiring harness (i. e. the harness will plug directory into the new ECM with a couple of pin location changes ... 3 or 4 pin moves, I think).<P>This computer is completely programmable and can use a 1 to 3 BAR MAP sensor (i.e. can/will work with 30 pounds of over pressure!!!)  It will work with the <B>STOCK</B> Fiero's V6 distributor.<P>See  <A HREF="http://www.haltech.com.au/Products/ECUs/E6GM/e6gm.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.haltech.com.au/Products/ECUs/E6GM/e6gm.html</A>  for what I have that is for sale.<P>I purchased it for $695 from a dealer in Miami, FL for a custom TPI setup I was going to use in a Fiero V8 swap.  That has been sold already (the custom TPI setup) but the Corvette guy couldn't spend the extra $$ for the Haltech so I still have it.  Best <B>REAL</B> offer gets it.<P>Paul||||reg||6
Z||000007||Slammed Fiero||05-15-2001||10:34 AM||||Just a side note:<P>I have heard lots of good things about the Haltech system from many autocrossers. Lots of guys in our club seem to be scratch building Fuel injection systems..<P>We have a AF-30 Haltech Air fuel meter in our 911 , very accurate when comapred to actual readouts provided by the motronic Programmable ECM...<P>May want to talk to TWM induction<BR> <A HREF="http://www.twminduction.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.twminduction.com/</A> <P>We got some parts for our 911 and Corvair from them . very knowledgeable and helpful.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1607505&a=12259132&p=48230089&Sequence=0&res=high">     <BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals..Autocrossed , Tracked , abused.||||reg||1
Z||000008||bcwawright||05-15-2001||10:51 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B>Just a side note:<P>I have heard lots of good things about the Haltech system from many autocrossers. Lots of guys in our club seem to be scratch building Fuel injection systems..<P>We have a AF-30 Haltech Air fuel meter in our 911 , very accurate when comapred to actual readouts provided by the motronic Programmable ECM...<P>May want to talk to TWM induction<BR> <A HREF="http://www.twminduction.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.twminduction.com/</A> <P>We got some parts for our 911 and Corvair from them . very knowledgeable and helpful.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hi Jonathan<BR>I asked you some questions about the Electromotive Tech system..especially mounting the triger at crank on a 2.8...any input. Just out of curiosity why didn't you put 18" wheels on your car?<BR>Thanks for any help<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Wannabe||05-15-2001||11:16 AM||||It surprises me that so few people seem to use these units as a solution to some of the problems encountered with common engine swaps. For example, it seems a stand alone unit would solve all of the computer problems associated with a Northstar/5sp swap or a 96+ 3800 s/c 5spd swap. Of course, it would take lots of work to get the best map programmed, but one it was done, you would have a completely funcional system with the potential for more upgrades. Cost may be a factor too, but when you are spending upwards of $10K for a swap, what's another $1000 or so to make it just right? ||||reg||1
Z||000010||terryk||05-15-2001||11:36 AM||||Ah, I didn't know the TEC was called a Tech 2. I know the Tech 2 as the scanner.<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000011||Will||05-15-2001||12:47 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Wannabe:<BR><B>Cost may be a factor too, but when you are spending upwards of $10K for a swap, what's another $1000 or so to make it just right? </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well... It wouldn't really be right. The only advantage most aftermarket systems have is that they are programmable. In almost every other area, they are dramatically inferior to OEM electronics. Does the TEC have an economy cruise mode? warm-up/cold-running compensation? individual cylinder bias? are the attack and decay rates of knock retard adjustable?<BR>We have a couple of cars running with '94-5 LT1 electronics, which have all of the above mentioned features and a few more, and can be reprogrammed using software from <A HREF="http://www.carputing.com" TARGET=_blank>www.carputing.com</A>  -- the best of both worlds.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Will||05-15-2001||12:49 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Wannabe:<BR><B>Cost may be a factor too, but when you are spending upwards of $10K for a swap, what's another $1000 or so to make it just right? </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well... It wouldn't really be right. The only advantage most aftermarket systems have is that they are programmable. In almost every other area, they are dramatically inferior to OEM electronics. Does the TEC have an economy cruise mode? warm-up/cold-running compensation? individual cylinder bias? are the attack and decay rates of knock retard adjustable?<BR>We have a couple of cars running with '94-5 LT1 electronics, which have all of the above mentioned features and a few more, and can be reprogrammed using software from <A HREF="http://www.carputing.com" TARGET=_blank>www.carputing.com</A>  -- the best of both worlds.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||terryk||05-15-2001||04:51 PM||||Yep, but when you can't change the cals, these look pretty good to some people.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Wannabe||05-15-2001||05:11 PM||||I wasn't refering to the the TEC II or stand alone systems in particular, but rather to the practice of band-aiding or fooling the exisiting engine management system to run in some diminished capacity because all of the sensor outputs can not be providied for. It's really amazing how much is available to get more lattitude from the factory computer, but so many just put up with the operating gaps left when you try to fool the system. The point is, there are lots of ways to get these engines to run properly, what is surprising is how many don't bother.||||reg||1
